WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.238
Every evening a bank builds a wall: its ninety-nine percent value at risk,

00:04.338 --> 00:07.581
a loss it expects to exceed on only one day in a hundred.

00:07.681 --> 00:10.923
Here is that wall for the S and P five hundred, built the

00:11.023 --> 00:15.144
textbook way, and nine years of losses rolling in. The promise was about

00:15.244 --> 00:18.838
twenty-three days over the wall. The sea delivered fifty-three.

00:20.033 --> 00:23.197
To build the wall we need two things: how rough the

00:23.297 --> 00:26.333
sea is today, and how tall its rare waves can be.

00:27.433 --> 00:31.161
First, how rough is the sea? A daily return sees only two

00:31.261 --> 00:35.123
prices, the open and the close. Here are two days that both

00:35.223 --> 00:38.615
end up half a percent: a calm one, and a stormy one.

00:38.715 --> 00:42.980
Their daily returns, and so their squared returns, are identical.

00:43.066 --> 00:46.542
Now sum the squares of the tiny one-minute moves as

00:46.642 --> 00:50.328
the day goes on. The calm day collects almost nothing;

00:50.428 --> 00:54.465
the stormy one collects twenty-five times more. That sum is

00:54.565 --> 00:59.162
realized volatility: a direct, model-free reading of the day's sea.

01:00.233 --> 01:04.510
Most days a bank has only closing prices. RiskMetrics then measures the

01:04.610 --> 01:08.949
sea from footprints: each day's squared return is pressed into the sand,

01:09.049 --> 01:12.833
and every evening all the older prints fade by six percent. The

01:12.933 --> 01:15.545
weight of a day halves in about eleven days.

01:16.633 --> 01:22.621
February twenty eighteen: a few deep prints, and tomorrow's volatility jumps at

01:22.721 --> 01:27.321
once. Then the walk goes on, and the deep prints slowly fade.

01:27.421 --> 01:31.945
There is no constant, so no level to return to: only memory.

01:33.033 --> 01:38.076
Is the wall too low because RiskMetrics misjudges the roughness? No. Divide

01:38.176 --> 01:42.190
each return by its forecast sigma and look at the left tail.

01:42.290 --> 01:46.510
The normal curve says the worst one percent starts at minus two

01:46.610 --> 01:50.487
point three three. The data say minus three point one two.

01:50.566 --> 01:54.073
So keep the same volatility, but build the wall from the

01:54.173 --> 01:58.131
data's own tail: a third higher. The overtopping days fall from

01:58.231 --> 02:02.639
fifty-three to twenty-one, and Kupiec's test is satisfied. The sea was

02:02.739 --> 02:06.375
measured right; the wall assumed waves of the wrong shape.

02:07.466 --> 02:11.810
The options market also forecasts the sea: the VIX. It moves with

02:11.910 --> 02:16.733
the volatility that follows, but sits above it on eighty-four percent of

02:16.833 --> 02:20.767
days. Investors pay a premium to be insured against storms.

02:21.866 --> 02:26.170
So: value at risk is a wall, and backtesting counts the days the

02:26.270 --> 02:30.849
sea came over it. Measure the sea well, from intraday data or fading

02:30.949 --> 02:35.872
footprints, and then build the wall from the tail the data actually have.
